# Python's hex/bin/oct take ARBITRARY PRECISION; pylib's are
# `function hex(n: Int64)`, so a value past a machine word had no matching
# overload and `hex(x)` simply failed to compile
# (feature-nilpy-hex-bin-oct-over-a-big-int).
#
# A PromoInt OVERLOAD in pylib cannot be written: a PromoInt PARAMETER cannot be
# handed to the promo runtime at all, and `shr`/`Integer(n)` on one are both
# broken, so the digit loop is unwritable in Pascal source
# (bug-a-promoint-shr-yields-nothing-and-a-machine-int-cast-yields-the-slot-address).
# So the frontend lowers a promo-typed argument to promocore's own
# PXXPromoToBase, which works off the bignum limbs directly — the same shape
# `**` uses for pypow_v.
#
# Bases 2/8/16 are all powers of two, so the conversion reads BIT GROUPS out of
# the byte buffer rather than dividing per digit. Octal is the one that does not
# nest inside a byte (3 does not divide 8), which is why the 2**70 oct row is
# here.
#
# A NATIVE-width argument still takes pylib's Int64 path untouched — the first
# group below — so both spellings must agree, since which one a program reaches
# depends only on whether its value happened to grow.
#
# Every expectation is CPython's.

# native path, unchanged
print(hex(255), oct(255), bin(255))
print(hex(0), oct(0), bin(0))
print(hex(-255), oct(-255), bin(-255))
print(hex(1), bin(1))

# arbitrary precision
big = 1180591620717411303424
print(hex(big))
print(oct(big))
print(bin(big))

huge = 1267650600228229401496703205376
print(hex(huge))

neg = -1180591620717411303424
print(hex(neg))
print(oct(neg))

# right at the machine-word boundary
w = 18446744073709551616
print(hex(w), oct(w))

d = 12345678901234567890
print(hex(d))

# the result is an ordinary string
s = hex(big)
print(len(s), s[0:2], s[2:6])
